WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.200
- I regret tremendously not
spending all my money on Google

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.300
in 2001 to 2004.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.530
When I rewound how I
built my dad's business,

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:11.310
it was because I was handed a full house.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.400
It was called Google Day
One, five cents a click.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.900
My CAC was 10 cents, my LTV was $8.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.913
I didn't understand.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.133
You got your perspective.

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:25.758
(audience cheering)

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:26.591
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:26.591 --> 00:00:27.580
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.850
Something that's become very obvious to me

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.800
only in the last couple of weeks

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.200
that maybe I'll pontificate
a little bit here, which is

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.130
I think the biggest reasons so many people

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:45.750
within the marketing landscape
do have disagreements

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:46.970
with some of my points of view

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.560
is because I really don't think

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.270
of myself as a marketer, for say.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.020
I no question think of myself far more

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.140
as a business operator who
happens to have a knack

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.140
or an intuition towards marketing,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.810
which has helped me throughout my career,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.280
but everything I believe in

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.240
is predicated on the final
result of what everybody

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.900
in this room is trying to achieve.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.370
I just think that marketing
and communications

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:15.210
is an incredible way to get there.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:16.043
Put it right there.

00:01:16.043 --> 00:01:16.876
Thanks, D-Rock.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.020
For a lot of you that don't
know my career, I was born

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.870
in the former Soviet Union, I
came to the US and my father

00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:28.610
grew from being a stock boy
to owning a small liquor store

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.210
in New Jersey and when I joined
that business, first I fell

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.510
in love with wine and wine
collecting, which was great

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:37.130
because it's always good if
you like what you're doing,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.390
but I built my dad's business
from a three to a $60 million

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.880
business very quickly in the late '90s

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.140
without any VC capital,
even without a credit line.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.560
When I think about how
remarkable that growth was,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.530
it was completely
predicated on practicality,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.923
which is really my North Star today.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.810
If I had the luxury to sit
and have dinner or breakfast

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.390
with you one-on-one for four
hours and we actually talked

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.570
about what you're trying to
achieve in your business,

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.360
so much of what I believe in,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.490
whether it's Facebook or
Instagram or content...

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.360
Text messaging is starting to
really be fascinating to me

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.510
because in the US and many
other markets for about a decade

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.560
we haven't allowed marketing
inside of our text numbers

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.590
and all of a sudden, I'm seeing
people willing to do that,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.790
which you can imagine is
attractive for us marketers

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:26.790
and business people.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.760
I think the biggest reason
I'm interesting and debating

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.900
in the marketing landscape
is I'm not a product or a son

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.630
of Nielsen ratings or
Datalogix or brand lift studies

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.803
or impressions.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.340
If you knew nothing about
marketing and you were a normal

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.210
business person, you wouldn't
be attracted to low CPMs.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.970
You would be attracted to high
CPMs because it would lead

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.170
to what you're trying to achieve.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.730
So, I sit in marketing
meetings from major clients

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.070
around the world, I sit at
conferences, events like this

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.620
and people pontificate
on data in the middle

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.080
that has nothing to do with
the end business result.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.230
That is my problem.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.740
That is why I'm an outlier.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.280
That's why I'm different.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.863
I'm not of the machine,
I'm of the business result.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.020
It's really interesting,
by only running businesses

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.370
for myself my whole life,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.480
I only care about long term
branding and marketing.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:26.920
A lot of times, people think about digital

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.950
as a short term sales funnel.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.330
I think of it as reverse.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.500
I think Facebook and Instagram
and thank you so much

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.370
for being a part of this, but
I think that they're doing

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.640
not a good job in expressing
how much branding is done

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.970
in their channels because
the conversion is so obvious

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:44.960
for math and sales.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.680
When I think about this
room, whatever it may be,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.900
the business result that
one's trying to achieve,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.400
I watch so many of your behaviors
globally in overspending

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.240
for A-list celebrities in Hollywood

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:57.997
that don't have the value they used to

00:03:57.997 --> 00:04:02.810
and overspending in quant-based
transactional Google

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:06.720
and Facebook behavior where
CAC and LTV are the religion

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.970
and branding is not.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.930
I watch a lot of things that
if I bought your business

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.850
or if I ran it, I wouldn't
believe in, but everything in

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:18.480
the system, everything that
the marketing industry has been

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.510
built on for the last
half century values things

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.350
that I just don't think
are mapping to reality.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.220
I'm fresh eyes to this industry.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.020
I've only been in an
environment where big companies

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.170
have been part of my
life for the last decade.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.030
Prior to that, it was
only entrepreneurship

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.130
and Silicon Valley.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.420
So, it's been an amazing journey
for me the last half decade

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:46.170
in watching what is put on
a pedestal in an environment

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:50.130
when you don't see every
dollar spent where it goes to,

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.420
what happens when you fall
too deep into just caring

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.423
about the value of every
dollar in the short term.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:00.420
To be honest, for my DNA,
it's a bizarro world.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.280
An industry that goes to the
South of France to celebrate

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.650
itself on subjective creative
and uses those awards

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:09.680
to justify hundreds of
millions of dollars in spend

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:10.603
is fascinating.

00:05:11.446 --> 00:05:12.580
(audience laughing)

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:17.090
It is and the giggles come from

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.113
a deep understanding of the truth.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:26.210
Here we are in 2019 whether
in the UAE, America, Europe,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.620
all over the world, you have
a game of two individuals.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.460
You have people who are spending money

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.490
and it's not their business
and you have people

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:36.510
that are spending money

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.780
and that's how they feed their family.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.910
And those two people, the
way they spend their money

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.370
and what they believe in
have never been more opposite

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.200
than today, and that's
what I'm fascinated by.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.610
The people that are
incentivized of the health

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.330
of the business in the short
and long term versus the people

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.870
that are incentivized on
the KPIs within the machine

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.650
that they have to navigate through.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:59.000
I deploy empathy.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.140
I used to judge a lot
of people in this room

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.970
on paper a decade ago.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.190
I thought I was smarter.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:05.710
I know I'm not smarter.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:07.790
I just know that I'm
playing a different game.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:10.100
I'm running a marathon in perpetuity.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:13.500
I put out content to be
historically correct, so I can trade

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.980
on reputation in a decade,
not on what pays my bills

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.110
in the short term.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.960
So, I have the advantage of the framework

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.990
that I've stumbled into
within this environment,

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.890
but it doesn't make it any less true.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.890
So, couple things if you leave
with anything, the one word

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.470
I would leave with if you're
in here today is volume.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:39.300
The sheer volume of content
that is needed by the businesses

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.700
in this room is staggering.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.533
It's staggering.

00:06:42.533 --> 00:06:45.180
If you get your above the line
creative agency to interpret

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.880
your TV and make a ton more
content for the internet,

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.450
if you have a digital shop
that does a lot of content,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:54.000
if you do publishing deals where
Conde or Hearst or Refinery

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.760
or PureWow are giving you content,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.300
if you build internal
capabilities for content,

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.970
if you have all four of
those things humming,

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.380
you're still 90% short on
how much content you need

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:07.110
in a 2019 world if you
understand how the Googles

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.920
and Facebooks and Snapchats actually work.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.030
If you are a deep practitioner
of the media capabilities

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:17.900
of 2019 across the biggest
platforms, which by the way,

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:21.523
in this region, have so much
attention, it's uncomfortable.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.450
If you really understand
that, you'll realize, my God,

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:30.450
I need 20,000, 8,000 unique
pieces of content for different

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:35.610
psychographic, demographic
individuals and as you all know,

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.560
even Vayner, which I'm trying
to move in the direction

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.820
of being the disproportionate
leader in quality

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.670
and quantity, there's no
engine in the world right now

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:47.610
that's even remotely close to the needs

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:48.610
that we actually have.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.040
So, that to me is the big one.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.930
That to me is in the
last six months the thing

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.810
that is absolutely
synthesized, which is my God,

00:07:55.810 --> 00:08:00.810
if you really did it perfect
and you really spent it

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:04.880
every penny the way I did for
my father's business perfect,

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.520
you need 20,000 pieces
of meaningful content.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.520
For a year, that's about 19,900
more than most people have.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:16.660
So, that I think will be the debate

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.730
over the next half decade.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:22.240
How do you have quality content
at scale to take advantage

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.200
of the grossly underpriced
media capabilities

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.973
of the YouTubes, the
Snapchats, the Facebooks

00:08:28.973 --> 00:08:31.160
and the Instagrams over
the next half decade,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.603
then consumer behavior will
change either to our hope,

00:08:37.068 --> 00:08:40.220
the prices become appropriate
like they did on Google search

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:43.990
or people's attention will
move on like it was on MySpace.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:44.823
That I don't know.

00:08:44.823 --> 00:08:46.120
I don't guess.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.370
I only trade on the day we live in.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.260
What I know is if you
look at the sheer data

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.410
of what's happening in
this region specifically,

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.740
and this is global outside
of mainland China and Russia,

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:01.740
if you look at the sheer
actual consumption, not a GRP

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.030
or an impression, which
is a potential reach,

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:06.780
but actual reach...

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.620
If you just look at
YouTube, Facebook, Instagram

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:12.870
and Snapchat, this is
the golden year to market

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.920
in this region and most people
are not taking advantage

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.150
of it because either A,
they've allocated dollars

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:21.690
to traditional places based
on reports of yesterday

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:24.740
or B, even if they are,
it's not successful

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:25.970
because they don't have enough content

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:28.180
to fill the pipes of
the media distribution.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.030
That to me is what's going on.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:32.720
- You mentioned about
how much of the content

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.370
is actually made for utility
or how much was actually made

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:36.310
for ourselves, right?

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.470
- How much is made for
them or for yourselves.

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.950
I think utility and entertainment
can be made for them.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.350
I think that if you look at
a lot of the communications

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.930
in this room, I'm sure if
you were to self audit,

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:49.200
you'd be surprised how
much is self serving.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.364
- Exactly, and that's the
point I wanna come to.

00:09:51.364 --> 00:09:52.940
There's a level of awareness
that companies need to have

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.210
in terms of what they're
doing inside the organizations

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:58.940
for their own ego and how
do you instill something,

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:01.380
a culture in an organization,
that they're actually aware

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.220
of what they're doing for
themselves versus for all?

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:05.290
- That's very hard.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:09.600
You do that by whoever runs
the company sets that tone.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.040
Every company's DNA is
predicated on the CEO.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.900
Whoever she or he is,
they're gonna dictate that.

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.670
So, if she or he are
disproportionately egotistical

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.390
around subjective creative,

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.450
the whole organization will go up that.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:25.660
I think that that's something
outside the pay grade

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.470
of marketing that is just
absolutely operational

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:32.090
throughout the entire world.

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:35.040
What is being held up
as a religion internally

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:35.943
is fascinating.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.550
Knowing who's in this room,
you're either mathed out

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:42.530
or you're arged out.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:45.980
There are very few organizations
that have a 50/50 balance.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:49.260
You either have a DNA internally
that disproportionately

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.100
wants celebrities from
Hollywood and wants a one minute

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:56.220
video that feels good or you're
disproportionately Google

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:00.230
and emailed out for CAC
and LTV and I'm fascinated

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:04.100
why most organizations haven't
found that 50/50 balance

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.360
and understanding how both
matter and then more importantly

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.940
how do we actually score within that.

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:12.400
Creative Scorecard is laughable.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:16.540
It's human subjectiveness or
reports that are so laughably

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:21.120
outdated or finally
awards or finally three

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:24.520
or four magazines that say
it's a good piece of creative.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.960
The way we judge is
meanwhile we live in a world

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:29.680
where you can put this
creative in a Facebook

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.720
or a YouTube environment and you can get

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.930
qualitative feedback at scale

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:36.490
and just listen to the customer instead.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:40.020
The problem is most people
start top down, not bottom up.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:44.760
So, you overspend so heavily
on one video that you're

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:48.210
at the mercy of reporting
or Ipsos or ACE testing

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:50.730
or all these things that are
just, again, if you don't know

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:52.830
anything about them, like
I didn't eight years ago

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.870
and you actually study...

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:57.160
First time I ever heard
award winning work leads

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.260
to business results, which
was like a big debate I heard

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:04.170
in the advertising world, I
just had two people on my team

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.950
in research and strategy
find out where that started.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.703
It was funded by agencies.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:12.793
Sounds right.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.540
I think those are the themes
of today, no question.

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:21.220
- And themes in terms of
risks, we're seeing this huge

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.050
disruption across lots
of different industries,

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:28.730
Uber, Airbnb, etc., how do
companies try to identify

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:31.160
the shifts that are gonna
come, these tectonic shifts

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:32.990
that are taking place and
what should they do about it?

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.420
- Well, I think everybody here

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.840
should take a very simple stance
like I did a long time ago,

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:40.713
which is that the internet
will eliminate the middle.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.170
If you actually understand
what the internet does

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.950
in its most basic form, it
eliminates anybody in the middle

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.143
that provides no value.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.330
That's the squeeze.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:54.100
So, for me, I think everybody
in this room needs to think

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:55.250
about a couple things.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:57.570
If the internet squeezes the middle,

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:58.700
that's how it plays out.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:02.030
Airbnb made so much sense
to me if you think about it.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.430
It's just inventory that exists

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.530
that the internet
connected two parties to,

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.740
mo different than Uber, no
different than everything else.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.330
Listen, if you're an airline,
obviously somebody has

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:18.560
to then own a plane and that becomes a cog

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.430
that's more expensive than other things,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:25.160
but rooms have less costs
involved as we've seen play out.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.150
And then you start
getting into experiences

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.830
and other variables,
but you can't just rely

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:31.640
on the utility part of that.

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:33.470
You've gotta layer it.

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:36.450
It's how I think about what's
happened with television.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:37.820
It's the reason I knew that commercials

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.368
were gonna be in trouble.

00:13:39.368 --> 00:13:44.080
It's not that I'm predicting,
it's every day that you see

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:47.760
Netflix and YouTube
consumption numbers go up,

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.060
that's a problem for a network television.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:54.630
And then you just live in
common sense, like consuming

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:58.670
a commercial during a television program

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:03.657
is a very wild rarity in 2019

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:06.880
when we all have mobile devices.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.420
I always laugh when people in the US say,

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:10.750
but sports commercials are good.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:12.580
Meanwhile, the data's
very black and white.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.960
The biggest spike on social media

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.190
is during the commercials
of major sporting events

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:19.540
because everybody wants to talk about

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:22.333
what LeBron or Messi or Tom Brady did.

00:14:26.168 --> 00:14:29.533
It just feels like one big
game of Inside Baseball

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.810
where there's a lot of
financial incentives

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:36.360
to hold up a facade that no longer exists

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.230
and unfortunately for the
biggest brands in the world,

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:42.040
the executives making
the financial decisions

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.760
are not incentivized
to do the right thing.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:47.370
They're incentivized to follow the scoring

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.150
that's been created internally.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:52.290
That's on the CEO's head.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.550
- Given the shifts that
you're talking about,

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.680
explain some of the other
steps that you would take

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:58.250
if you were a marketer,

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.390
given the environment you just painted.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:01.223
- You know, it's funny.

00:15:01.223 --> 00:15:04.270
A lot of people razz on me
for my absolute statements,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.280
but I think of marketing like poker.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.170
If you have the best hand, you go in.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:12.070
I'm a boy and I say a
boy because I was a kid

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.560
when this happened, I am unbelievably...

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:19.980
I regret tremendously not
spending all my money on Google

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.090
in 2001 to 2004.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.320
When I rewound how I
built my dad's business,

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.090
it was because I was handed a full house.

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:30.180
It was called Google Day
One, five cents a click.

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.690
My CAC was 10 cents, my LTV was $8.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:35.950
I didn't understand because I didn't know.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:37.040
I didn't have experience yet.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:38.200
I didn't know how to quantify it.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:39.610
It seemed normal to me.

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:41.260
I was digitally a native.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:42.860
This seemed like it made sense.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.730
Why wouldn't everybody do this?

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.230
And then it went away.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.800
And then 2007, 8, 9 happened.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:52.290
So, for me, we're sitting
in that moment right now.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.520
Instagram Story Ads are
so grossly underpriced,

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:56.863
it's almost uncomfortable.

00:15:59.140 --> 00:16:00.260
Actually, this is amazing.

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:01.460
Maha, you in here?
- [Maha] Yes, I'm right here.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.090
Well, you know this.

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:06.800
In four minutes, in five
days, my follow account

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.783
on my Gary Vee Arabic page went
from 900 people to 30,000--

00:16:10.783 --> 00:16:11.616
- [Maha] 700 people.

00:16:11.616 --> 00:16:15.790
- 700 people to 30,000 because
we found a single piece

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.770
of content that I converted
into Arabic from a piece

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:24.770
of content that is achieving
1.9 cent follows on Instagram.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.590
If you understand the friction
to get somebody to follow you

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.660
based on an impression,
think about how inexpensive

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.980
I'm getting in front of
every Arabic speaking person

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:42.030
that is on Instagram to
get 1.9 cent follows.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.360
So, when I found out on a $200
spend that that was working,

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:47.690
I just poured all my
money into and by the way,

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:50.110
I'm gonna pour all my
money into it in perpetuity

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:54.240
cause I wanna extract the
underpriced nature of the media

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.340
and I figured out how to
create the volume of content

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:00.040
that broke through to eventually
get me a 1.9 cent follow

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:02.310
because we've gotten on
our 30th piece of content,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.670
we got there, not on our first.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.950
People are making one video
and then putting it on YouTube

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:10.530
and Facebook and then
saying, does it work or not?

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:13.070
We're not producing creatively
natively contextually

00:17:13.070 --> 00:17:15.170
to the platforms that
we're advertising on.

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:18.730
We're using television
mentality for the internet.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:21.223
It's remarkably wrong.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:26.640
Not even Vayner and let alone
all the traditional agencies

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:29.130
are not in a position to
create the work needed

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.560
for the realities of the marketplace.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:32.790
- Talk us through a
couple of things there.

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:34.700
You mentioned Instagram and
obviously, there's a shift

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:35.900
towards video.

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:38.600
Talk us through creating
great content for that

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.170
and also in an increasingly mobile world,

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:44.390
can you build a brand on mobile alone?

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:46.410
- Of course you can because
you can build a brand

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:49.650
on attention and the sheer
amount of attention on mobile

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:50.970
is extraordinary.

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:55.000
As a matter of fact, to
me when I look at outdoor

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:55.900
and I like outdoor.

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.390
There was a nice little digital outdoor

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:58.460
of me speaking today.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:01.280
I get excited about that, I
like outdoor, but I don't like

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:04.380
outdoor pricing in a world
where while I saw my little

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.810
outdoor piece, I looked around
the cars on the way here

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.480
and every single person not driving

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:09.830
was looking at their phone.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:13.810
Even the drivers, to your point.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:15.600
I'm a very simple guy.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:18.640
Tell me where the attention
is, tell me what the cost is

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:19.810
associated with it.

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:21.270
Do I think that's right?

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:25.390
Can I then create creative to
fill it at a low enough cost

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.710
that I can test seven, 10, 15?

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:31.480
This is not A-B testing, this
is A to quadruple Z testing.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:35.270
So, for me, it's about
producing quality content

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:36.350
at a low enough cost.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:39.210
Quality being contextual and empathetic,

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.870
not high production.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:43.310
Let me say that very slow
cause it's super important.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:46.150
Quality being contextual
to the distribution

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:49.630
and being empathetic.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:53.310
You have to know how to overlay
a sticker or an animated GIF

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:55.550
on an Instagram story to
get people's attention

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:59.340
cause that's native to that
platform, not doing matching

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:03.180
luggage of your TVC for a
short form 15 second video

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:04.023
for YouTube.

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.670
That's what big companies do.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.790
- Yeah, talk us through how
companies are getting it wrong.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.040
You mentioned there what we can do--

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:11.490
- They start with television.

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:13.443
You've already lost.

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:19.650
You make a TVC or a brand
campaign and then you ask somebody

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:22.630
whether it's that agency or
somebody else or internally

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:26.330
to cut it down for Facebook
and Instagram and YouTube.

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:28.950
You're already in trouble
for a couple reasons.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:33.830
By nature, brand campaigns
and television are vanilla

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.420
cause you're trying to reach
everybody with the reach

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:37.860
of that campaign.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:41.080
If you start from the bottom,
you can go after ex-pats

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:43.320
from the UK versus ex-pats from the US.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.550
As you can imagine, if you know
that that's who you're gonna

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:48.143
reach, your message is
gonna be slightly different.

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:53.280
I'm filming right now
everywhere I go, me and D-Rock.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:54.810
I'm standing outside with
these beautiful views

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:56.470
and we're here a little
early and I'm saying,

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:59.660
hey Snapchat, find out
why I'm here at the UAE.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:03.080
I'm literally filming for the distribution

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:06.300
just by saying, what's up
Facebook, as my opening line

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.350
disproportionately
increases the shareability

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:10.620
and earned media because
I made the creative

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.200
native to the distribution.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.760
I didn't make one video
that's gonna be super glossy

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:15.970
and then try to use everything.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:18.780
Everybody views these
channels as distribution.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:21.313
I view them as contextual
creative platforms.

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.440
I'm not trying to get
reach that isn't achieved

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.290
because I didn't make content for it.

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:31.470
Everybody's playing in a GRP
and reach world without having

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.550
a common sense layer over it
of are you actually getting

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:37.780
that reach and then number
two, does your content speak

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:38.860
to that reach.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:43.820
We didn't have that with
television, but now I can attack

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:46.110
the seven million people in this market

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.950
in 31 cohorts that matter.

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:50.600
Men and women are different.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.460
18 year olds are different
than 49 year olds.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:54.790
Making a million dollars
a year is different

00:20:54.790 --> 00:20:56.700
than making $40,000 a year.

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.530
We do not take that
into account in creative

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:01.070
because creative costs are too high.

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:04.620
The machines for creative
aren't built for scale.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:07.030
That is the rub in our industry.

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.820
The media people know this.

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:11.040
When I announced our
framework that leads off

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.000
of what I'm talking about in September

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:14.900
where I started my company meeting with,

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.420
I'm gonna put us out of business
before somebody else does,

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:18.630
it was the media people

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.580
that most liked this creative strategy.

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:24.280
The creative people like to
hold onto the political power

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.610
of making a subjective call,
not letting the market.

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:31.960
It's reactions quant and qual
dictate creative adjustment.

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:34.140
So, it's a very, very
interesting time right now.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.150
We have way too many
conversations in the ad world

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:37.630
about the media side of things.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:39.900
We are not having the proper conversation

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:43.200
of the subconscious bias against
the wrong creative process

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:44.193
for this story.

00:21:46.122 --> 00:21:48.705
(ethnic music)

